component necessitates a focus on predictable results rather than seeking cooperative outcomes.\n\n\n**Analyzing Causal Independence**\n\nI'm now diving into the implications of Causal Decision Theory (CDT) within this framework. Specifically, I'm analyzing the act of choice as an intervention ($\\text{do}(A)$). It's crucial that I'm keeping the background causal state fixed, since my actions cannot influence the other prisoner's choice. I'm treating the other prisoner's act as causally independent. My utility calculations hinge on this, where $p$ is the probability the other prisoner sends a letter. If I keep quiet, the expected years are $2 + 3p$, and if I send a letter, it's $4p$.\n\n\n","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":0},{"type":"reasoning.encrypted","data":"","format":"google-gemini-v1","index":1}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8982,"completion_tokens":546,"total_tokens":9528,"cost":0.008784,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.008784,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.0067365,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.0020475},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":510,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}